  "about": "Every mutant was applied to the file as it is at base_commit, which the change that adds this record does not change: it adds only tests, to the test module of the file's own tests (agent_import_identity_tests.rs). killer is the first test nextest reported failing (it stops at the first failure, with tests running in parallel, so another failing test may be reported first on another run). killer_at says where that test function exists: base, or this_commit for a test the change that adds this record adds. A test that exists at base may still kill only through cases that change adds to it: before_this_change gives the verdict at base where it differs. Each mutant that survived its filtered run at base was run again at base against every bridge test (the whole_set run, with no filter and nextest's --no-fail-fast, the approval stand-in example built) before it was called a survivor. Every survivor left at this_commit is listed in accepted_survivors with its reason: an equivalent one is marked equivalent. A hand-written mutant's mutation id is the first 32 hex digits of the SHA-256 of its edits in order, each as its from text, a NUL byte, its to text and a NUL byte, in UTF-8. Each edit's from text occurs exactly once in the file at base_commit, and applied_to names the file it was applied to. A tool mutant is named as cargo-mutants --list names it, by line and column. A hand-written mutant whose edit does not compile is recorded as unviable with the compiler's reason, not as caught. The hand-written mutants (I01 to I69) change, one at a time: the domain string bridge.mcp.import.v1 (another version, empty, upper-case first letter, another namespace, hyphens for dots); the members and order of the tuple (batch id and transaction id swapped, the domain last or in the middle, each of the three members dropped, a member given twice in place of another, an extra empty fourth member, the batch id or the transaction id lower-cased, trimmed or empty); the encoding (NUL-, colon- and un-joined text, pretty-printed JSON, the Rust Debug text, a JSON object, and the serialization error read as empty bytes); the digest (SHA-512, SHA-256 twice, the first input byte skipped); the slice range and buffer (the second half, shifted by one, fifteen and seventeen bytes, a fifteen-byte buffer, a buffer that starts at ones); the UUID builder and its use of the bytes (version 4, no stamp, little-endian fields, versions 5 and 3, the bytes reversed, a constant in place of the digest, and the version or the variant overwritten after the build); identity_batch_id (always the line's own batch id, an empty id in its own arm, the arms exchanged, an empty id for an amendment); attribution_tag (the batch-v1 arm with the line's own batch id, with the raw transaction id, with the arguments swapped, with an empty transaction id, and with the id written without hyphens, in upper case or as a URN; the unversioned arm with the derived id of either batch id and with an empty string; the two arms exchanged; the batch-v1 arm never taken); and the serde rename of the scheme (kebab, camel, lower, upper, screaming-snake and Pascal case, and the rename dropped). Not mutated: the derive lists, the visibility of the items, the doc comments, the test module's attribute, the expect message's text, and the enum's one variant (it has no other); the domain, the members and the digest range are mutated by the changes listed here, not by every possible one; and the rename is mutated by the six other cases listed, not by every spelling. Twelve edits (I07, I09, I24, I26, I35, I44, I50, I54, I57, I58, I59 and I63) were changed to an equivalent edit of the same kind (a neighbouring spelling of the same change: a trailing comma in the tuple, an array for a tuple, to_string_pretty(...).into_bytes() for to_vec_pretty, unwrap_or(Vec::new()) for unwrap_or_default(), [0u8; 15] for [0; 15], with_version on one line, .map(|_| \"\").unwrap_or(...) for map_or, String::default() for String::new(), as_str(), &String::default() for \"\", as_simple() for simple(), and the whole serde attribute line for its argument), because the first spelling gave a mutation id holding a run of eight or more decimal digits, which the privacy scan refuses. In every run the file was byte for byte as at base_commit, checked after each restore (and, for the tool, after the run); the restore was a copy, never a rename. In the tool, hand and whole_set runs the test module was as at base_commit; in survivors_again it is as this change leaves it.",
